==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-play on.ogg|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{q|also 'play upon'}} To [[exploit]] (a [[potential]] [[weakness]] or [[vulnerability]]) by [[exacerbate|exacerbating]].
#: ''to '''play on''' someone's fears''
# {{q|also 'play upon'}} To exploit (a [[double meaning]], [[similarity]] in [[sound]], etc.) for [[humorous]] or [[creative]] [[effect]].
#: {{ux|en|This joke '''plays on''' the fact that 'thirsty' sounds similar to 'Thursday'.}}
# {{q|also 'play upon'}} To [[impinge]] upon in a [[recur]]ring or [[nag]]ging way.
#: ''to '''play on''' one's mind, or conscience''
# {{q|also 'play upon'}} To [[frequently]] [[allude]] to or [[return]] to as a [[theme]].
#: {{ux|en|The pamphlet '''plays on''' the idea that all social conflict is class-based.}}
# {{lb|en|cricket|of a [[batsman]]}} to [[hit]] the [[ball]] into his own [[wicket]], thus being [[out]] [[bowled]]
# {{lb|en|intransitive|sports}} To [[let]] the [[game]] [[continue]] after a [[foul]] has been [[commit]]ted, because the [[situation]] is [[advantageous]] to the [[team]] who would be [[award]]ed a foul.
# {{lb|en|transitive|sports|_|such as soccer}} To [[cause]] (an [[opposing]] [[player]]) to be [[onside]] rather than [[offside]] due to one's [[position]] on the [[field of play]].
#: {{ux|en|The ref said that Smith was offside, but I think that Jones '''played''' him '''on'''.}}
# {{&lit|en|play|on}}
#: {{ux|en|I knew I was losing, but I '''played on''' anyways.}}
#: {{ux|en|sunlight '''playing on''' her brown hair}}

